{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-67455",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=8.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=-5.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1)=8$ and $r=-5$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-5)^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=8\\cdot (-5)^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
